findings.body:
While removing the redundant clone in `WryWebviewDispatcher::delete_cookie`, the message variant also changed from `DeleteCookie` to `SetCookie`. The head now routes both `set_cookie` and `delete_cookie` to `SetCookie`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-runtime-wry/src/lib.rs" lines="1715-1721"}

The public path is `Webview::delete_cookie` (::file{file="crates/tauri/src/webview/mod.rs"} around line 2097), which forwards straight into this dispatcher. A caller who deletes a cookie gets the cookie written into the webview store instead. The `DeleteCookie` arm of the event-loop handler (`crates/tauri-runtime-wry/src/lib.rs` around line 3737) becomes unreachable.

The clone removal is right, and `into_owned()` on a by-value `Cookie` is what `set_cookie` already does. Only the variant is wrong. The existing tests don't exercise this mapping, so nothing would have caught it.

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a one-token fix, but it has to land before merge. A regression test for the dispatch mapping would also prevent a repeat.

The renamed `window_features` body has irregular spacing in its two builder calls, which `cargo fmt --check` will flag:

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/webview/webview_window.rs" lines="1315-1322"}

The PR also moves the `inner_size` call ahead of `position`. The builder setters write independent attributes, so the order probably doesn't matter functionally. But the PR description doesn't mention the move, and it isn't a rename, so a reviewer can't tell whether it was intentional.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/webview/webview_window.rs" lines="1316-1322"}
if let Some(size) = features.size() {
  self.window_builder = self.window_builder.inner_size(size.width, size.height);
}

if let Some(position) = features.position() {
  self.window_builder = self.window_builder.position(position.x, position.y);
}

If the order was meant to change, say why in the commit or PR description. If not, restore the original order (position, then size).

The example's new window label changes from `new-{number}` to `new-window-{number}`. Nothing in the repo depends on the old label, and the PR description doesn't mention the change, so it's unclear why it was made.

::code{file="examples/api/src-tauri/src/lib.rs" lines="76-91"}

The `let mut created_window_count` binding is also unnecessary. `AtomicUsize::fetch_add` takes `&self`, so `mut` does nothing and rustc emits an `unused_mut` warning. The original code had the same `mut`, but since the PR is already moving this block, it's the right place to drop it.

The base example called `with_window_features` unconditionally, but that method is only defined on macOS, Windows, Linux, and the BSDs. The move into `#[cfg(all(desktop, not(test)))]` brings the example back in line with what the method supports.

::code{file="examples/api/src-tauri/src/lib.rs" lines="74-103"}

The `desktop` alias is `!mobile`, so it is broader than the method's OS list. Some non-mobile targets outside that list would still fail to compile, but that was already true before this PR.
